; pi4WifiScan.pi4 - bring the CYW43455 up and print the networks it can
; see. The list of SSIDs on the serial console is the deliverable.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; This is the artefact tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py runs for the scan
; path. Its output format is load-bearing: the gate reads values back
; out of the serial log by label, so a label changed here changes the
; gate.
;
; ======================================================================
; ARM THE DEADMAN. `deadman 15` BEFORE `run`. EVERY TIME.
; ======================================================================
; CORRECTED 2026-08-27, AND THE OLD TEXT IS QUOTED BELOW BECAUSE IT WAS
; FOLLOWED AND IT COST A HAND POWER CYCLE.
;
; This header used to read "RUN IT WITH THE DEADMAN OFF", on the
; arithmetic that the payload can take eleven seconds against a sixteen
; second counter and that "the failure mode if it goes over is a reset
; in the middle of a firmware upload - which costs a power cycle and
; tells you nothing."
;
; THE SECOND HALF OF THAT SENTENCE IS BACKWARDS, and it is the half that
; matters. A deadman expiry does not cost a power cycle - it is the one
; thing that AVOIDS one. Read RunAt in RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4 (the
; deadman is armed at line 446 and SafetyWatchdogStop runs at line 635):
; the watchdog is armed immediately before the jump and stopped
; immediately after, so a payload that hangs never reaches the stop, the counter
; runs out, and THE BOARD RESETS ITSELF BACK INTO THE FIRMWARE. It comes
; back on its own. Unarmed, the same hang wedges the board and needs a
; hand on the mains switch, because U-Boot is gone from the stick and
; there is no other way back (RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/
; pi4Install.pi4 lines 1-52).
;
; The arithmetic is still true and is still worth having:
;
;   bring-up      609,312 bytes uploaded and READ BACK for verification,
;                 so about 1.2 MB across the bus       ~1 to 2 s
;   NVRAM         1,748 bytes, likewise                 negligible
;   HT clock, F2  bounded polls                         < 1 s
;   the scan      #SCAN_BUDGET_MS below                 up to 8 s
;                                                       ------------
;                                                       up to ~11 s
;
; Eleven against fifteen - the ceiling is reported as 15, not 16, because
; a deadman must never fire before it said it would - is four seconds of
; margin. If a run does overrun it, the cost is a reset and a re-run,
; which is cheap. The cost of the other choice was measured on
; 2026-08-26 and again on 2026-08-27: a manual power cycle, twice.
;
;     pmf> deadman 15
;     pmf> map              <- it prints `stage a file at <stage>`. An
;                              image is the same bytes at any --load-addr.
;     pmf> run <stage>
;
; A bench-practice note written on 2026-08-26 - the same day as the text
; this replaces - said to arm the deadman before every payload, directly
; contradicting it. Two documents disagreed and the wrong one was the one
; sitting in the file open beside the board. That is why this correction
; is a paragraph and not a deletion.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE PREAMBLE THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL
; ======================================================================
; SdioInit REFUSES WITHOUT A BASE CLOCK and says so: on a BCM2711 the
; SDHCI Capabilities register reads back zero in the base-clock field,
; so the number has to come from the property mailbox instead. Every
; diagnostic on this bus needs these three calls, in this order, before
; SdioInit:
;
;     MailboxInit()
;     hz = MailboxClockRate(1)         ; clock id 1 is EMMC, NOT 12
;     SdioSetBaseClockKhz(hz / 1000)
;
; Clock id 1 is EMMC and feeds the controller at $FE300000. Id 12 is
; EMMC2 and feeds the micro-SD slot. They are different clocks with
; different rates and picking the wrong one produces a bus that almost
; works.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHERE THE FIRMWARE COMES FROM
; ======================================================================
; NOT FROM HERE. This payload does not touch the filesystem. The three
; blobs are read off the boot stick into DRAM by Anvil's `load` before
; the payload runs, and this program is handed nothing but addresses:
;
;     load BRCMFW.BIN  1000000     609309 bytes  crc32 $B8B12CED
;     load BRCMNV.TXT  1200000       2074 bytes  crc32 $2BDBDDF6
;     load BRCMCLM.BLB 1300000       2676 bytes  crc32 $346D4B26
;
; BRCMFW.BIN and BRCMCLM.BLB are Firmware/CYW43455/ in this repository.
; BRCMNV.TXT is the board's own NVRAM and is NOT - it is calibration
; and identity data - so #NV_LEN and #NV_CRC below describe one board's
; file. tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py --scan builds a copy of this program
; with those two constants naming the NVRAM text it uploads instead.
;
; THE THIRD LINE IS NEW ON 2026-08-27 AND IT IS THE POINT OF THE DAY.
; The CLM blob was on the stick and was never loaded, so the PHY had no
; regulatory data, so every escan came back BCME_NOTUP (-4) even though
; WLC_UP itself returned 0. tools/anvil_putfile.py put all three files
; there and records the 8.3 mapping; the long names do not fit a short
; directory entry, which is why BRCMCLM.BLB and not .clm_blob.
;
; A PAYLOAD TAKES NO ARGUMENTS, so it cannot be told what it was given.
; It CRCs each buffer against the constant below and refuses on a
; mismatch. That guard is not ceremony - it caught a real bug on the
; first run of the bring-up payload. If a CRC line prints WRONG, the
; `load` did not happen, loaded a different file, or loaded it
; somewhere else, and nothing below the CRC check would be meaningful.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT EACH STOPPING POINT MEANS
; ======================================================================
;   stops at "crc"          the DRAM does not hold what this expects.
;                           Re-run Anvil's load; nothing is wrong with
;                           the radio.
;   stops at "SdioInit"     the bus. Check the mailbox preamble above.
;   stops at "Cyw43Attach"  the chip. This is the ground already proven
;                           on 2026-08-26; a failure here is a
;                           regression, not new territory.
;   stops at "F2 ready"     THE FIRMWARE DID NOT BOOT. Image and NVRAM
;                           both verified, so look at the reset vector
;                           and at whether the ARM left reset.
;   stops at "wifi up"      one of three ioctls inside Cyw43WifiUp was
;                           refused. The trace lines name which: 13/14
;                           are the CLM chunks, 15/16 the clmload_status
;                           query, 3/4 the event mask and 5/6 WLC_UP.
;                           A return of -5 is the special one - the blob
;                           uploaded and the firmware rejected it, and
;                           "clm status" below carries its reason.
;   "scan started" then     the transport can send but not receive, or
;   nothing                 the event mask never took. Look at the
;                           frame counters - rx frames 0 with a clean
;                           scan start is a receive-side fault, and rx
;                           frames non-zero with events 0 is the mask.
;   networks 0, DONE        the firmware completed a scan and saw
;                           nothing. That is a REAL ANSWER and a
;                           different failure from a timeout. Check the
;                           antenna before the code.
;
; ======================================================================
; STOPS INSIDE THE TRANSPORT, WITH NO RESULT LINE AT ALL
; ======================================================================
; Everything above this line reports itself: each call prints a row with
; its result, so "the last row printed" names the failing call. The
; transport does not work that way. Cyw43WifiUp is one call containing
; three ioctls, a poll loop and two chunking loops, and its result line
; is printed only if it comes back - so a hang in there produces
; "-- transport --" and then silence, which names nothing.
;
; So the transport ANNOUNCES ITSELF. probe_Trace below prints a line
; before every step it is about to attempt and drains the UART before
; attempting it, and the last such line is the failing step:
;
;     -- transport --
;       .. 1  Cyw43WifiUp entered
;       .. 2  transport bookkeeping reset
;       .. 3  SENDING bsscfg:event_msgs      <- stopped here on 2026-08-27
;
; THAT EXACT STOP HAS HAPPENED AND ITS CAUSE IS WORTH KNOWING, because
; it will happen again in some other file. Step 3 died inside
; Cyw43SetEventMask on an unaligned 32-bit store: the iovar name is
; seventeen characters and a NUL, so the u32 after it lands two bytes
; off a word boundary, and with the MMU off every data access is
; Device-nGnRnE where an unaligned access is an Alignment fault - with
; no vector installed, and therefore no message. Fixed at source; the
; long argument is at cyw43_PutLE32 in Lib/cyw43.pi4.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43_rx_glom.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Where Anvil's `load` put the two files, and what they must contain.
;
;  THE CRCs ARE THE CONTRACT. They were measured on the board by reading
;  each file back off the stick, not by CRCing a serial transfer, so
;  they cover the whole path from the stick to DRAM.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#FW_ADDR = $01000000
#FW_LEN  = 609309
#FW_CRC  = $B8B12CED

#NV_ADDR = $01200000
#NV_LEN  = 2074
#NV_CRC  = $2BDBDDF6

; The CLM blob. THE CRC WAS MEASURED ON THE BOARD, by loading
; BRCMCLM.BLB off the stick and running Anvil's own crc32 over it, and
; it agrees with zlib.crc32 of Firmware/CYW43455/
; brcmfmac43455-sdio.clm_blob on the host - two independent
; implementations of the same polynomial, which is what makes the number
; worth carrying.
;
; $01300000 IS CLEAR OF EVERYTHING ELSE. The NVRAM ends at $012008 1A,
; the cook workspace is at $01210000 and the verify scratch at
; $01220000, so there is a megabyte of daylight before this. It is
; inside Anvil's low payload window (00400000 to 07EFFFFF), which is
; checked by `load` and not by this file.
#CLM_ADDR = $01300000
#CLM_LEN  = 2676
#CLM_CRC  = $346D4B26

; Working buffers, placed after the two blobs. The NVRAM cook needs
; length + 8 and the verify scratch is brcmfmac's own MEMBLOCK.
#NV_WORK      = $01210000
#NV_WORK_MAX  = 4096
#SCRATCH      = $01220000
#SCRATCH_LEN  = 2048

; Mailbox clock id 1 = EMMC. U-Boot v2025.01
; arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/include/mach/mbox.h line 229. NOT 12, which is EMMC2's.
#CLOCK_ID_EMMC = 1

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The scan's budget, and it is the CALLER's, deliberately.
;
;  A scan ends on an EVENT, never on a delay. Cyw43ScanPoll returns
;  RUNNING for as long as nothing has arrived, and this program decides
;  when to give up - because "no completion event in eight seconds" and
;  "completed with zero networks" are different failures with different
;  causes, and a library that timed out internally would report them
;  identically.
;
;  Eight seconds is generous: a full dual-band active scan is commonly
;  three to five. See the deadman arithmetic in the header for why it is
;  not more.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SCAN_BUDGET_MS = 8000
#SCAN_SLICE_MS  = 50


Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

; PrintNl and PrintDec ARE NOT DEFINED HERE. uart.pi4 already has both
; (lines 1155 and 1119) and defining them again is a duplicate-procedure
; error rather than a shadow, which is the compiler being helpful.
Procedure Line(*name, v.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec(v)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure HexLine(*name, v.i, digits.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  $")
  PutHexN(v, digits)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_Count - a count that is allowed to say it never happened.
;
;  A NEGATIVE VALUE MEANS NOT COMPARED and prints as those words. This
;  convention came out of the 2026-08-26 bench run, where a control
;  printed "0" while both of the reads it was comparing had failed and
;  nothing had been walked. Zero out of zero is arithmetically true,
;  reads as a pass, and was the most misleading line in the log. Every
;  count in this program that can be skipped goes through here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE TRANSPORT INSTRUMENT.
;
;  cyw43.pi4 cannot print - a library never includes a library - so it
;  announces each transport step through a callback and this is the
;  handler. See THE TRACE SEAM in cyw43.pi4 for the codes.
;
;  THE UartDrain() IS THE WHOLE POINT AND IT IS NOT TIDINESS. The
;  announcement is made BEFORE the dangerous call, so if that call never
;  returns, this line is the last thing the board will ever say and it
;  has to be OUT OF THE CHIP before the call is attempted. Without the
;  drain it sits in the PL011 transmit FIFO, the CPU blocks or faults
;  inside the call, nothing ever pushes the FIFO out, and the one line
;  that was supposed to survive the hang dies with everything else.
;
;  That was learned twice on 2026-08-26 and paid for again on
;  2026-08-27, when the payload printed "-- transport --" and then
;  nothing at all - and the cause turned out to be an alignment fault
;  three instructions into Cyw43SetEventMask that this instrument would
;  have named in one line.
;
;  A NUMBER AND A NAME, NOT ONE OR THE OTHER. The number survives a
;  rename of the text; the text is what gets read at two in the
;  morning. Printing both costs nothing and means a log filed today is
;  still readable when the codes have moved.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i probe_StepName(step.i)
  Select step
    Case 1
      ProcedureReturn "Cyw43WifiUp entered"
    Case 2
      ProcedureReturn "transport bookkeeping reset"
    Case 3
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING bsscfg:event_msgs"
    Case 4
      ProcedureReturn "event mask returned"
    Case 5
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING WLC_UP"
    Case 6
      ProcedureReturn "WLC_UP returned"
    Case 7
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING the escan request"
    Case 8
      ProcedureReturn "escan request returned"
    Case 9
      ProcedureReturn "no credit - draining to earn one"
    Case 10
      ProcedureReturn "pushing the request frame at F2"
    Case 11
      ProcedureReturn "WAITING for the reply"
    Case 12
      ProcedureReturn "the wait returned"
    Case 13
      ProcedureReturn "SENDING one clmload chunk"
    Case 14
      ProcedureReturn "every clmload chunk was accepted"
    Case 15
      ProcedureReturn "ASKING clmload_status"
    Case 16
      ProcedureReturn "clmload_status answered"
    Case 20
      ProcedureReturn "poll starting"
    Case 21
      ProcedureReturn "poll gave up on its budget"
    Case 22
      ProcedureReturn "F2 read - the fixed first 64 bytes"
    Case 23
      ProcedureReturn "F2 read - the rest of the frame"
    Case 24
      ProcedureReturn "F2 block-mode chunk"
    Case 25
      ProcedureReturn "F2 byte-mode chunk"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "unknown step - see THE TRACE SEAM in cyw43.pi4"
EndProcedure

Procedure probe_Trace(step.i)
  UartWriteStr("  .. ")
  PrintDec(step)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(probe_StepName(step))
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

Procedure probe_Count(*name, v.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  If v < 0
    UartWriteStr("NOT COMPARED - the step that would have produced it did not run")
  Else
    PrintDec(v)
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  crc32 over a DRAM buffer - the ordinary reflected CRC-32, polynomial
;  $EDB88320, both inversions.
;
;  TABLE-DRIVEN, WITH THE TABLE BUILT AT RUN TIME FROM THE POLYNOMIAL.
;  That is the whole of the trick and it is worth a paragraph, because
;  the obvious two options are both worse:
;
;    * BIT BY BIT is what this was first, and it is eight shifts per
;      byte. On the board that is milliseconds and nobody would care.
;      In the offline gate it is the single most expensive thing in the
;      run - 609,309 bytes times eight - and it made a gate that should
;      take a minute take twenty-five.
;
;    * A TYPED-OUT 256-ENTRY TABLE is eight times faster and is 256
;      constants nobody can check by eye against the polynomial they
;      came from. This project does not carry numbers like that.
;
;  Building the table from the polynomial at start-up costs 2048
;  iterations once, keeps $EDB88320 as the ONLY constant in the whole
;  routine, and leaves the per-byte loop at one shift and one lookup.
;  Checkable and fast, rather than one or the other.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CRC32_POLY = $EDB88320
Global Dim crcTab.l[256]
Global crcTabReady.i = 0

Procedure Crc32Init()
  Define i.i
  Define k.i
  Define c.i
  If crcTabReady = 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  For i = 0 To 255
    c = i
    For k = 0 To 7
      If (c & 1) = 1
        c = (c >> 1) ! #CRC32_POLY
      Else
        c = c >> 1
      EndIf
    Next
    crcTab[i] = c
  Next
  crcTabReady = 1
EndProcedure

; THE TABLE BASE AND THE CURSOR ARE HOISTED OUT OF THE LOOP, and that
; is not micro-optimisation for its own sake. This runs 609,309 times
; per call and twice per run; leaving `@crcTab[0]` inside the loop makes
; the compiler recompute an array base address every byte. On the board
; that is invisible. In the offline gate, which executes every one of
; those instructions in Python, it was the difference between a gate
; that takes a couple of minutes and one that takes twenty.
Procedure.i Crc32(*p, n.i)
  Define c.i
  Define i.i
  Define idx.i
  Define tab.i
  Define q.i
  Crc32Init()
  tab = @crcTab[0]
  q = *p
  c = $FFFFFFFF
  For i = 1 To n
    idx = (c ! PeekA(q)) & $FF
    ; PeekN, not the .l element: .i is 64 bits here and a .l read
    ; sign-extends, so a table entry with bit 31 set would come back
    ; negative and poison every byte after it.
    c = PeekN(tab + (idx << 2)) ! ((c >> 8) & $00FFFFFF)
    q = q + 1
  Next
  ProcedureReturn (~c) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure


Procedure.i Main()
  Define ok.i
  Define hz.i
  Define i.i
  Define j.i
  Define r.i
  Define crc.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define slices.i
  Define stop.i
  Define done.i
  Define found.i
  Define ssid.i
  Define n.i

  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("=== pi4 CYW43455 scan ===")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 1 - what is in DRAM
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- the blobs, as loaded --")
  PrintNl()

  HexLine("firmware at      ", #FW_ADDR, 8)
  Line("firmware bytes   ", #FW_LEN)
  crc = Crc32(#FW_ADDR, #FW_LEN)
  HexLine("firmware crc32   ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #FW_CRC
    HexLine("  EXPECTED       ", #FW_CRC, 8)
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - Anvil's load did not put BRCMFW.BIN here.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at crc ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  crc = Crc32(#NV_ADDR, #NV_LEN)
  HexLine("nvram crc32      ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #NV_CRC
    HexLine("  EXPECTED       ", #NV_CRC, 8)
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - Anvil's load did not put BRCMNV.TXT here.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at crc ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; THE CLM IS CRC'd LIKE THE OTHER TWO AND FOR A SHARPER REASON. The
  ; firmware image is verified again by read-back after upload, so a bad
  ; one is caught twice. The CLM is not: clmload_status reports whether
  ; the firmware could PARSE what it assembled, which a blob that is
  ; wrong in the middle can still pass. This CRC is the only check that
  ; the 2676 bytes about to be handed to the radio are the 2676 bytes
  ; that came out of RPi-Distro.
  Line("clm bytes        ", #CLM_LEN)
  crc = Crc32(#CLM_ADDR, #CLM_LEN)
  HexLine("clm crc32        ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #CLM_CRC
    HexLine("  EXPECTED       ", #CLM_CRC, 8)
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - Anvil's load did not put BRCMCLM.BLB here.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  Type:  load BRCMCLM.BLB 1300000")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at crc ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 2 - the bus. The mailbox preamble is not optional; see the header.
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- bus --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = MailboxInit()
  Line("mailbox init (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the mailbox ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  hz = MailboxClockRate(#CLOCK_ID_EMMC)
  Line("EMMC clock Hz (id 1)", hz)
  If hz <= 0
    UartWriteStr("  the firmware would not report the EMMC clock, and")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  SdioInit refuses to guess a base clock.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the clock ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  SdioSetBaseClockKhz(hz / 1000)
  Line("base clock kHz   ", hz / 1000)

  ok = SdioAssertWlOn()
  Line("WL_ON asserted (want 1)", ok)

  ok = SdioInit()
  Line("SdioInit (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the bus ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = SdioEnableFunction(1)
  Line("enable F1 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at function 1 ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 3 - the chip
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- chip --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = Cyw43SetIo(@SdioCmd52Read, @SdioCmd52Write, @SdioCmd53Read, @SdioCmd53Write)
  Line("Cyw43SetIo (want 1)", ok)
  ok = Cyw43SetBlockIo(@SdioCmd53ReadBlocks, @SdioCmd53WriteBlocks)
  Line("Cyw43SetBlockIo (want 1)", ok)

  ok = Cyw43Attach()
  Line("Cyw43Attach (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the chip ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  HexLine("chip id          ", Cyw43ChipId(), 4)
  Line("chip rev         ", Cyw43ChipRev())
  Line("F1 block size    ", Cyw43F1BlockSize())
  Line("F1 byte-mode max ", Cyw43ByteMax())
  Line("F2 block size    ", Cyw43F2BlockSize())

  ok = Cyw43SetBlockMode(1)
  Line("block mode on (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 4 - the firmware
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- firmware --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = Cyw43PrepareDownload()
  Line("Cyw43PrepareDownload (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped before the download ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  HexLine("ram base         ", Cyw43RamBase(), 8)
  Line("ram size bytes   ", Cyw43RamSize())

  ok = Cyw43UploadFirmware(#FW_ADDR, #FW_LEN, #SCRATCH, #SCRATCH_LEN)
  Line("Cyw43UploadFirmware (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    HexLine("  first bad addr ", Cyw43BadAddr(), 8)
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the image ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  HexLine("reset vector     ", Cyw43ResetVector(), 8)

  ok = Cyw43UploadNvram(#NV_ADDR, #NV_LEN, #NV_WORK, #NV_WORK_MAX, #SCRATCH, #SCRATCH_LEN)
  Line("Cyw43UploadNvram (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the nvram ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Line("nvram after cooking", Cyw43NvramBytes())

  ok = Cyw43Start()
  Line("Cyw43Start (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped releasing the ARM ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = Cyw43HtClock()
  Line("Cyw43HtClock (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the HT clock ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = Cyw43AnnounceHost()
  Line("Cyw43AnnounceHost (want 1)", ok)

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5 - F2 READY. THIS IS THE FIRMWARE SAYING IT BOOTED.
  ;
  ; There is no mailbox, no magic word and no version read. The host
  ; enables function 2 and polls CCCR IORx for bit 2; that bit going
  ; from 0 to 1 is the whole signal. Before the upload it reads 0 and
  ; that is CORRECT.
  ; ====================================================================
  ok = SdioEnableFunction(2)
  Line("enable F2 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  F2 NEVER CAME READY - the firmware did not boot.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at F2 ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 6 - the transport
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- transport --")
  PrintNl()

  ; THE INSTRUMENT GOES ON HERE, not at the top, because everything
  ; above this line already reports itself: each call has a result and
  ; a printed row, and a failure above stops with a named message. The
  ; transport is the part with nowhere to stop - three ioctls, a poll
  ; loop and two chunking loops under that, all inside one call that
  ; either returns a number or does not return.
  ;
  ; SECOND ARGUMENT 0 = COARSE. The fine points sit inside cyw43_Poll
  ; and cyw43_F2Xfer, which run thousands of times a second; printing
  ; and draining at each one does not merely make a long log, it changes
  ; the timing enough that the F2 FIFO overruns and the scan loses
  ; results. Pass 1 only when a call is already known to hang and the
  ; question is which loop inside it - and expect the scan itself to be
  ; unusable in that run.
  Cyw43SetTrace(@probe_Trace, 0)

  ; THE CLM GOES IN AS AN ARGUMENT rather than as a call of its own, and
  ; the reason is Cyw43TransportReset. That reset zeroes the SDPCM
  ; sequence and the BCDC request id and lives at the top of
  ; Cyw43WifiUp, so anything this program sent "before Cyw43WifiUp"
  ; would be sent with numbers the firmware then watches go backwards.
  ; The library owns the ordering; see Cyw43WifiUp's header.
  ; #CYW43_EVMASK_SCAN, NOT JOIN. This instrument's whole value is that
  ; every transport counter reconciles against the frames a clean scan
  ; produces, and the join mask lets ten more event types in - see the
  ; argument at Cyw43SetEventMask. The join has its own payload.
  r = Cyw43WifiUp(#CLM_ADDR, #CLM_LEN, #CYW43_EVMASK_SCAN, 2000)
  Line("Cyw43WifiUp (want 0)", r)
  ; THE CLM ROWS ARE PRINTED ON EVERY PATH, pass or fail, because they
  ; are the diagnosis. chunks 3 of 3 with status 0 is the good answer;
  ; chunks 2 with a negative return names the chunk that was refused;
  ; chunks 3 with a positive status means the radio took every byte and
  ; then rejected the blob, which is a different bug entirely.
  Line("  clm chunks sent", Cyw43ClmChunks())
  probe_Count("  clm status (want 0)", Cyw43ClmStatus())
  If r <> 0
    Line("  ioctl status   ", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    Line("  rx frames      ", Cyw43RxFrames())
    If r = -5
      UartWriteStr("  -5 is CLMBAD: every chunk was accepted and the firmware")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  then refused the assembled blob. The bytes in DRAM CRC'd")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  correctly, so suspect the chunk flags before the file.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at wifi up ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 6b - WHAT THE RADIO SAYS IT IS NOW HOLDING
  ;
  ; None of these three is required to scan. All three are one ioctl
  ; each and each answers a question that was previously argued about
  ; rather than measured, so they are sent once, here, after the CLM and
  ; after WLC_UP where every one of them is legal.
  ;
  ; A REFUSAL HERE IS NOT FATAL AND MUST NOT BE. These are instruments,
  ; and an instrument that aborts the run it was measuring is worse than
  ; no instrument. Each prints its own verdict and the scan goes ahead.
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- what the radio says --")
  PrintNl()

  ; "clmver" - the first HUMAN-READABLE proof the CLM went in, as
  ; distinct from clmload_status saying the transfer completed. Several
  ; \n-separated Key: value lines. Printed byte by byte with everything
  ; unprintable turned into a dot and newlines turned into spaces,
  ; because this is firmware-supplied text and it is not this program's
  ; job to let it move the cursor.
  r = Cyw43GetClmVersion(2000)
  Line("clmver rc (want 0)", r)
  If r = 0
    UartWriteStr("  clmver  ")
    ssid = Cyw43ReplyPtr()
    n = Cyw43ReplyLen()
    ; A While AND NOT A For, because this loop has to STOP at the first
    ; NUL and assigning to a For's counter to break out of it is not
    ; something this language promises anything about. The reply buffer
    ; is 128 bytes of which the string uses maybe sixty; the rest is
    ; whatever the firmware left there.
    j = 0
    While j < n And PeekA(ssid + j) <> 0
      If PeekA(ssid + j) >= 32 And PeekA(ssid + j) <= 126
        UartWrite(PeekA(ssid + j))
      Else
        UartWrite(32)
      EndIf
      j = j + 1
    Wend
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; "country" - READ, NEVER WRITTEN, and the argument for that is in
  ; Cyw43GetCountry's header in cyw43.pi4. The short version: brcmfmac
  ; does not set it at bring-up, this board's NVRAM has no ccode line at
  ; all, and the firmware picks a domain by itself. This row is here so
  ; that what it picked stops being a matter of opinion.
  ;
  ; The struct is country_abbrev[4], le32 rev, ccode[4].
  r = Cyw43GetCountry(2000)
  Line("country rc (want 0)", r)
  If r = 0
    ssid = Cyw43ReplyPtr()
    UartWriteStr("  country abbrev  ")
    For j = 0 To 1
      If PeekA(ssid + j) >= 32 And PeekA(ssid + j) <= 126
        UartWrite(PeekA(ssid + j))
      Else
        UartWrite(46)
      EndIf
    Next
    UartWriteStr("   ccode  ")
    For j = 8 To 9
      If PeekA(ssid + j) >= 32 And PeekA(ssid + j) <= 126
        UartWrite(PeekA(ssid + j))
      Else
        UartWrite(46)
      EndIf
    Next
    PrintNl()
    ; rev is a SIGNED 32-bit field and -1 is a legal, meaningful value
    ; ("unspecified"), so it is sign-extended before printing. Read
    ; unsigned it would print 4294967295, which looks like garbage and
    ; is in fact the normal answer.
    n = PeekA(ssid + 4) | (PeekA(ssid + 5) << 8) | (PeekA(ssid + 6) << 16) | (PeekA(ssid + 7) << 24)
    If n >= $80000000
      n = n - $100000000
    EndIf
    Line("  country rev    ", n)
  EndIf

  ; "scan_ver" - v1 or v2, settled by one ioctl instead of by argument.
  ;
  ; brcmfmac decides purely on WHETHER THE NAME EXISTS: feature.c:184-201
  ; enables BRCMF_FEAT_SCAN_V2 for any error other than
  ; -BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED, and BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED is 23 (feature.c:20).
  ; So status -23 means v1 and ANYTHING ELSE means v2. Our escan request
  ; is v1 and byte-for-byte the Pico W's, which has been scanning for
  ; months - this row is what turns "probably right" into a fact.
  r = Cyw43ScanVerProbe(2000)
  Line("scan_ver rc      ", r)
  Line("scan_ver status  ", Cyw43ScanVerStatus())
  Line("scan_ver value   ", Cyw43ScanVer())
  If Cyw43ScanVerStatus() = -23
    UartWriteStr("  status -23 is BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED: this firmware does")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  NOT know scan_ver, so escan v1 is correct - which is")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  what this driver sends.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  status is NOT -23, so brcmfmac would enable SCAN_V2 and")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  send brcmf_escan_params_v2_le instead. See cfg80211.c")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  lines 1451-1466. This driver still sends v1.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; "sup_wpa" - WHO RUNS THE SUPPLICANT. The same existence test as
  ; scan_ver above, on the name feature.c:341 uses, and the answer
  ; decides whether WPA2 costs two hundred lines of ioctls or a host
  ; PBKDF2-SHA1 and a hand-written four-way handshake. Asked here rather
  ; than in the join payload because this instrument already runs on
  ; every bench session and the answer is wanted BEFORE anything is
  ; written against it. Cyw43SupWpaProbe carries the full argument.
  r = Cyw43SupWpaProbe(2000)
  Line("sup_wpa rc       ", r)
  Line("sup_wpa status   ", Cyw43SupWpaStatus())
  Line("sup_wpa value    ", Cyw43SupWpa())
  Line("sup_wpa present  ", Cyw43SupWpaPresent())
  If Cyw43SupWpaPresent() = 0
    UartWriteStr("  status -23 is BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED: NO firmware supplicant.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  The HOST must do PBKDF2-SHA1 4096 and the EAPOL four-way")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  handshake itself. That is a much larger job - see")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  Cyw43SupWpaProbe in cyw43.pi4.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  NOT -23, so brcmfmac would set BRCMF_FEAT_FWSUP: the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  FIRMWARE can run the supplicant. Set bsscfg:sup_wpa = 1,")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  hand it the passphrase, and it does the handshake.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 7 - the scan
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- scan --")
  PrintNl()

  r = Cyw43ScanStart(0, 2000)
  Line("Cyw43ScanStart (want 0)", r)
  If r <> 0
    Line("  ioctl status   ", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped starting the scan ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; The guard timer is HERE and not in the library. See #SCAN_BUDGET_MS.
  ; TWO COUNTERS, NOT ONE. `slices` counts what actually ran and is what
  ; gets printed; `stop` is the loop's exit flag. Using the slice count
  ; itself to break the loop - setting it to the budget on completion -
  ; makes the printed number ALWAYS equal the budget, which is a row
  ; that looks like a measurement and is a constant. The first run of
  ; this program printed "slices used 160" for a scan that finished in
  ; six.
  done = -1
  slices = 0
  stop = 0
  budget = #SCAN_BUDGET_MS / #SCAN_SLICE_MS
  While stop = 0 And slices < budget
    r = Cyw43ScanPoll(#SCAN_SLICE_MS)
    slices = slices + 1
    If r = 2
      done = 0
      stop = 1
    EndIf
    If r = 3
      done = 1
      stop = 1
    EndIf
  Wend

  Line("slices used      ", slices)
  Line("slice budget     ", budget)
  If done = -1
    UartWriteStr("  NO COMPLETION EVENT within the budget. That is NOT the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  same as a scan that found nothing - see the header.")
    PrintNl()
  ElseIf done = 0
    UartWriteStr("  scan completed, status SUCCESS")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  scan completed, status ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43EventStatus())
    UartWriteStr(" - see fweh.h lines 114-129")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 8 - THE DELIVERABLE
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- networks --")
  PrintNl()

  found = Cyw43ScanCount()
  Line("networks found   ", found)
  For i = 0 To found - 1
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    ; The index, right-padded to two so the columns line up.
    If i < 10
      UartWrite(32)
    EndIf
    PrintDec(i)
    ; THE CONTROL CHANNEL, and the chanspec's centre channel beside it
    ; when they differ - which on a 40 or 80 MHz AP they always do. The
    ; first real scan on this board printed "ch 58" for an 80 MHz
    ; network whose control channel is 52, because this column used to
    ; be the chanspec's byte. 58 is not a channel anything sits on.
    ; cfg80211.c:3348 is the ruling; the long note is in cyw43_ParseBss.
    UartWriteStr("  ch ")
    n = Cyw43ScanChannel(i)
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(32)
    EndIf
    PrintDec(n)
    If Cyw43ScanChanspecChannel(i) <> n
      UartWriteStr("/c")
      PrintDec(Cyw43ScanChanspecChannel(i))
    Else
      UartWriteStr("    ")
    EndIf
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    n = Cyw43ScanRssi(i)
    If n > -100
      UartWrite(32)
    EndIf
    PrintDec(n)
    UartWriteStr(" dBm  ")
    If Cyw43ScanPrivate(i) = 1
      UartWriteStr("enc  ")
    Else
      UartWriteStr("open ")
    EndIf
    UartWriteStr(" ")
    For j = 0 To 5
      PutHexN(Cyw43ScanBssidByte(i, j), 2)
      If j < 5
        UartWrite(58)
      EndIf
    Next
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    ; THE SSID IS UNTRUSTED TEXT FROM THE AIR and is printed byte by
    ; byte with everything unprintable replaced, rather than handed to
    ; a string printer. A hidden network's SSID is all NULs and a
    ; malicious one can be any 32 bytes at all; neither should be able
    ; to move the cursor or end the line.
    ssid = Cyw43ScanSsid(i)
    n = Cyw43ScanSsidLen(i)
    If n = 0
      UartWriteStr("<hidden>")
    Else
      For j = 0 To n - 1
        If PeekA(ssid + j) >= 32 And PeekA(ssid + j) <= 126
          UartWrite(PeekA(ssid + j))
        Else
          UartWrite(46)
        EndIf
      Next
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
  Next
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 9 - what the transport saw, whether or not it worked
  ;
  ; EVERY COUNTER HERE IS A DIFFERENT BUG. "networks 0" with rx frames 0
  ; is a receive-side fault; with rx frames high and events 0 it is the
  ; event mask; with bad headers high it is the framing. One number
  ; would have hidden all three.
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- transport counters --")
  PrintNl()
  Line("rx frames        ", Cyw43RxFrames())
  Line("bad headers      ", Cyw43RxBadHdr())
  Line("too big          ", Cyw43RxTooBig())
  Line("too short        ", Cyw43RxShort())
  Line("other channels   ", Cyw43RxOther())
  Line("other events     ", Cyw43EvOther())
  Line("bad event prefix ", Cyw43EvBadIsland())
  ; ONE PER SCAN IS THE NORMAL READING AND IT IS NOT A FAULT. A frame
  ; twelve bytes long whose SDPCM data offset is also twelve is a header
  ; with no payload behind it - the firmware handing the host a credit
  ; update with nothing attached. It was captured on the bench on
  ; 2026-08-27 and its bytes are transcribed in cyw43_RxFrame; until
  ; then it was landing in "bad event prefix" above, which is why every
  ; clean scan on this board used to report exactly one anomaly. That
  ; row is now expected to be ZERO and this one is not.
  Line("empty events     ", Cyw43EvEmpty())
  Line("credit stalls    ", Cyw43Stalls())
  Line("table overflow   ", Cyw43ScanOverflow())
  ; NON-ZERO IS THE NORMAL READING HERE AND IS NOT A FAULT. An escan
  ; reports one PARTIAL event per beacon or probe response the radio
  ; heard, so one access point arrives several times; the table merges
  ; them by BSSID and this is how many merged. The first bench run that
  ; exposed the behaviour printed five networks for two real ones -
  ; three folds. "networks found" plus this is the number of PARTIAL
  ; results the firmware delivered, and that is the number that has to
  ; square with "rx frames" above.
  Line("duplicates folded", Cyw43ScanDuplicates())
  ; TWO CROSS-CHECKS, AND THEY NO LONGER MEAN THE SAME THING.
  ;
  ; "sync id mismatch" is still a pure layout alarm: non-zero means the
  ; field is not where this driver thinks it is.
  ;
  ; "channel mismatch" IS NOT, since 2026-08-27. It counts records whose
  ; ctl_ch and chanspec channel differ, which is EVERY 40 or 80 MHz
  ; access point and is completely normal - the first scan on this board
  ; raised two of them and both were the same 5 GHz network. It is only
  ; an alarm when it is non-zero and the list above shows no 5 GHz rows
  ; (nothing above channel 14) and no "/c" suffixes. Read it against the
  ; channel column, never on its own.
  Line("channel mismatch ", Cyw43ChanMismatch())
  Line("sync id mismatch ", Cyw43SyncBad())
  Line("bss_info length  ", Cyw43BssLength())
  PrintNl()

  UartWriteStr("=== done ===")
  PrintNl()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Main()
